<SEOArticle title="Workplace Ergonomics Guide & Desk Setup Checklist" description="Use this workplace ergonomics guide to improve desk setup, monitor position, keyboard and mouse placement, movement breaks and remote-work habits." canonical="/workplace-wellness/workplace-ergonomics/" eyebrow="Workplace Wellness" heading="Workplace ergonomics for healthier, more comfortable workdays." lead="A good workstation reduces avoidable strain, but no chair or desk can make staying still all day ideal. Use this guide to improve the setup and build more position changes into the workday." {faqs} {toc} {sources} secondaryLabel="Desk Mobility Routine" secondaryHref="/workplace-wellness/desk-mobility-routine/">
 <p><strong>Ergonomics means fitting the job to the person.</strong> For computer work, that involves more than buying an expensive chair. Work surface height, screen position, input devices, lighting, task duration and movement opportunities all affect the experience.</p>
 <h2 id="checklist">Office and home workstation checklist</h2>
 <h3>Chair and lower body</h3>
 <ul><li>Feet are supported by the floor or a stable footrest.</li><li>Knees and hips are in a comfortable position without pressure behind the knees.</li><li>The chair supports the lower back without forcing an uncomfortable curve.</li><li>Seat depth leaves room between the front edge and the lower legs.</li><li>Frequently used controls can be reached while supported.</li></ul>
 <h3>Desk, keyboard and mouse</h3>
 <ul><li>Shoulders can remain relaxed rather than elevated.</li><li>Elbows stay near the body during common tasks.</li><li>Wrists can remain generally in line with the forearms.</li><li>The mouse is close enough that the arm does not repeatedly reach forward.</li><li>Frequently used objects are within easy reach.</li></ul>
 <h3>Monitor and visual setup</h3>
 <ul><li>The primary screen is generally in front of the user rather than far to one side.</li><li>The top portion of the display is at a comfortable viewing height.</li><li>Text is large enough to read without leaning toward the screen.</li><li>Glare from windows and lights is reduced.</li><li>Multiple monitors are arranged around which screen is used most.</li></ul>
 <div class="callout"><strong>Use the checklist as a starting point, not a posture test.</strong> Bodies and work tasks differ. Make one adjustment, use it and reassess comfort rather than forcing a textbook position.</div>
 <h2 id="movement">Movement breaks matter as much as setup</h2>
 <p>OSHA notes that working in the same posture or sitting still for prolonged periods is not healthy, even when the posture begins in a good position. Change position frequently throughout the day.</p>
 <div class="grid two">
  <div class="card"><h3>Change position</h3><p>Alternate supported sitting, standing and other comfortable positions when the task allows.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><h3>Walk briefly</h3><p>Use a short walk to refill water, speak with a colleague or reset between focused tasks.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><h3>Move relevant areas</h3><p>Hands, shoulders, upper back, hips and ankles may benefit from gentle movement after remaining still.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><h3>Vary the task</h3><p>When possible, alternate typing, reading, calls, meetings and other work instead of repeating one demand for hours.</p></div>
 </div>
 <p>Try linking movement to events that already occur: after a meeting, before lunch, when switching projects or after completing a focused work block. This is easier to remember than relying on motivation.</p>
 <h2>Standing desk guidance</h2>
 <p>A sit-stand desk can make position changes easier, but standing is not a workout and can become uncomfortable when prolonged. Begin with short standing periods, keep the screen and input devices usable in both positions and use supportive footwear appropriate to the workplace.</p>
 <h2 id="remote">Remote-work ergonomics</h2>
 <p>Laptops are convenient because the keyboard and screen are attached; that same design makes an ideal long-duration setup difficult. When working for extended periods, consider an external keyboard and mouse plus a stable way to raise the laptop screen.</p>
 <ul><li>Avoid working for hours from a couch or bed when a supported alternative is available.</li><li>Use household items only when they create a stable, safe setup.</li><li>Make video-call placement secondary to physical comfort.</li><li>Check lighting and screen visibility throughout the day.</li><li>Create separation between work and recovery when space allows.</li></ul>
 <h2 id="company">How employers can build an ergonomics program</h2>
 <ol><li><strong>Assess:</strong> identify common tasks, employee concerns and higher-risk work patterns.</li><li><strong>Educate:</strong> teach employees how to adjust equipment and change position.</li><li><strong>Provide:</strong> create a clear process for requesting equipment or an assessment.</li><li><strong>Support movement:</strong> ensure workload and culture allow reasonable breaks and position changes.</li><li><strong>Evaluate:</strong> review participation, unresolved barriers and patterns in employee feedback.</li></ol>
 <p>An ergonomics webinar without authority to adjust equipment or work practices is incomplete. Pair education with a practical response process.</p>
 <h2>When to seek additional help</h2>
 <p>Employees should know how to report discomfort and request support. Numbness, weakness, significant swelling, symptoms following injury or pain that persists or worsens should not be managed only with an online checklist.</p>
 <h2>Connect ergonomics with workplace wellness</h2>
 <p>Ergonomics addresses the fit between work and people. A broader <a href="/corporate-wellness/">corporate wellness program</a> can add guided movement, strength, recovery, nutrition and stress-management resources without using wellness activities to excuse unsafe work design.</p>
